th one meld of 3 tiles, total 13. So I need to discard one tile to have 12 tiles before drawing.  \n\nNow, analyzing potential:  \n- T6 triplet is a Pung (could be a meld if exposed, but currently concealed).  \n- I have potential sequences in W suit: W2, W4, W6, W7, W8. Possible Chow: W6-W7-W8 (I have W6, W7, W8, that's a Chow!). So that's another meld. Also W2-W3-W4? I have W2 and W4, missing W3. W4-W5-W6? I have W4 and W6, missing W5.  \n- B suit: B2 and B4, not close.  \n- T3 is lone.  \n\nSo currently, I have:  \n- Exposed Chow: [T3, T4, T5]  \n- Concealed Pung: T6 T6 T6  \n- Concealed Chow: W6, W7, W8  \nThat's three melds. Remaining tiles: W2, W4, B2, B4, and one T3? Wait, T3 is in hand? But the exposed Chow uses T3, so the T3 in hand is separate? That seems odd. Possibly the hand tiles list includes T3 even though it's part of the meld? That would be double-counting. Let's assume the hand tiles are correct: I have a T3 in hand. But then the exposed Chow [T3, T4, T5] would require a T3, so maybe I had two T3s originally and used one for the Chow? That's plausible. So I have one T3 left.  \n\nSo after accounting for the two melds (T6 Pung and W6-W7-W8 Chow), I have left: W2, W4, T3, B2, B4. That's 5 tiles. I need one meld and a pair. Possible melds:  \n- Pair: any two identical. I have no pairs currently.  \n- Melds: could be Chow from W2-W3-W4 (need W3), or B2-B3-B4 (need B3), or pair + another tile.  \n\nSo I need to improve toward a hand. Discard options:  \nValid actions: Play W2, W4, W6, W7, W8, T3, T6, B2, B4. But T6 is a triplet, so discarding one would break the Pung. That's bad because T6 Pung is a meld. So discard T6 is not good. Similarly, W6, W7, W8 form a Chow, so discarding any would break it. So discard from W6, W7, W8 is not good. So better discards are from the loose tiles: W2, W4, T3, B2, B4.  \n\nConsidering tile safety: Look at game history. Opponents have discarded many tiles. My previous discards: J2, F4, W1, B9, then after Chi T4, I played F4, T4, B7, F4. So I've discarded F4 multiple times, and others have discarded F3, J3, etc. Unshown tiles counts: F3:0 (all gone), F4:1 left, J3:1 left, etc.  \n\nFor tile efficiency: I need to form melds. Which tiles are more useful?  \n- W2: can form Chow with W3-W4 or W1-W2-W3. I have W4, so W2 needs W3. Unshown W3: 3 left.  \n- W4: can form with W2-W3 or W4-W5-W6. I have W6, so W4-W5-W6 needs W5. Unshown W5: 3 left.  \n- T3: can form with T1-T2-T3 or T2-T3-T4 or T3-T4-T5. But T4 and T5 are used in my exposed Chow? Actually, T4 and T5 are in the meld, so they are not in my hand. So T3 is less useful alone.  \n- B2: needs B1-B2-B3 or B2-B3-B4. I have B4, so B2-B3-B4 needs B3. Unshown B3: 3 left.  \n- B4: needs B2-B3-B4 or B4-B5-B6. I have B2, so B2-B3-B4 needs B3.  \n\nSo all need one middle tile. T3 is less connected because T4 and T5 are used. But I could form a pair with T3 if I get another T3. Unshown T3: 3 left.  \n\nAlso, I have potential for All Simples (no terminals/honors) since my tiles are 2-8 mostly. And maybe Half Flush if I focus on one suit? But I have mixed suits.  \n\nScoring potential: Currently, I have:  \n- Concealed Pung (T6) could give Two Concealed Pungs if I get another concealed pung.  \n- All Simples possible if I avoid terminals/honors.  \n- Possibly Mixed Triple Chow? Not really.  \n- Chicken Hand if no other patterns.  \n\nBut I need at least 8 points. So I need to aim for higher patterns. Perhaps I can go for Full Flush or Half Flush? I have mostly W suit tiles: W2, W4, W6, W7, W8, plus the W6-W7-W8 Chow. If I discard all non-W tiles, I could aim for Full Flush (all same suit). But I have T6 Pung and T3 in hand, and an exposed Chow in T suit. The exposed Chow is in T suit, so Full Flush is impossible because meld is exposed in another suit. Half Flush is possible: honor tiles and one suit. I have no honors in hand, but I could get some. But Half Flush is 6 points, need additional 2 points.  \n\nAlternatively, All Pungs: I have one Pung (T6), need three more pungs. Unlikely.  \n\nBetter to aim for a hand with multiple small patterns.  \n\nConsidering discard safety: I should discard tiles that are less likely to give opponents what they need. Looking at history:  \n- Next player has meld [F3, F3, F3], so they have a Pung of F3. They discarded W9, J3, T9, J1, T6, then Peng F3, played B7, B3, J1, W1. They seem to be collecting honors? But F3 is gone.  \n- Across player has melds: [T9, T9, T9] and [W1, W2, W3]. They discarded J3, F1, then Peng T9, played W1, B8, F1, W5, W8, T8, Chi W2, played B2. They have Chow in W1-2-3, so they might be collecting W suit. They discarded W5 and W8, so maybe not.  \n- Previous player: no melds, discarded J3, T8, B8, T4, F3, B8, F3, B7, T1.  \n\nSo W tiles: W1, W2, W3 are in across player's meld, so they are safe. W5, W8 discarded by across. W9 discarded by next. W4, W6, W7 not discarded much. B tiles: B2 discarded by across, B3 discarded by next, B7 discarded by many, B8 discarded by previous and across. T tiles: T4 discarded by me and previous, T6 discarded by next, T8 discarded by across and previous, T9 melded by across.  \n\nThus, safer discards might be tiles that have been discarded already: B2 (discarded by across), T3 (not discarded recently, but T4 and T6 discarded), W2 (in meld of across, so safe?), but W2 is in across player's meld, so they already have it, but others might need it. Actually, since across has a Chow with W2, they are unlikely to need another W2 for Pung, but others might.  \n\nGiven I need to keep tiles that can form melds, I should keep W2 and W4 because they can combine with W3 or W5 to form Chow. T3 is less useful because T4 and T5 are used. B2 and B4 are similar.  \n\nBut I also have the T6 Pung, which is valuable. So I should not discard T6.  \n\nSo candidate discards: T3, B2, B4. Among these, T3 is a terminal? T3 is not terminal (terminal is 1 or 9). So it's simple.  \n\nMaybe discard T3 because it's isolated and the T suit is already used in my exposed Chow, so less chance for more T melds.  \n\nBut let's check potential patterns: If I discard T3, my hand becomes: W2, W4, W6, W7, W8, T6x3, B2, B4. Then I have two melds (T6 Pung and W6-W7-W8 Chow) plus exposed Chow. I need one more meld and a pair. The remaining: W2, W4, B2, B4. I could pair W2 with another W2, or B2 with another B2, etc. And form a Chow from W2-W3-W4 or B2-B3-B4.  \n\nIf I discard B2 or B4, similar.  \n\nBut if I discard W2 or W4, I lose connectivity in W suit.  \n\nSo best discard among loose tiles: T3, B2, B4. Which is safest? B2 was just discarded by across player, so it might be safe. But also, B4 has not been discarded much. T3 has not been discarded by me.  \n\nLooking at unshown tiles: T3: 3 left, B2: 3 left, B4: 4 left.  \n\nI think discard T3 is reasonable because it's less connected to my other tiles.  \n\nBut let's consider the possibility of going for Half Flush: If I discard all B and T tiles, I need to draw W tiles. But I have an exposed Chow in T suit, so Half Flush requires honors and one suit. Since my exposed Chow is in T suit, Half Flush would require all other melds and pair to be W suit or honors. That's difficult.  \n\nSo probably just aim for a simple hand with All Simples, Concealed Pung, etc.  \n\nGiven the need for at least 8 points, I might need to rely on Self Drawn, All Simples, Concealed Hand, etc. But I already have an exposed meld, so Concealed Hand is impossible.  \n\nScoring patterns I can potentially get:  \n- All Simples (2 points)  \n- Two Concealed Pungs (2 points) if I get another concealed pung.  \n- Dragon Pung? No.  \n- Seat Wind? I'm East Wind (F1), but I have no F1.  \n- Prevalent Wind is West (F3), but F3 is all gone.  \n- Mixed Double Chow? Possibly if I get two chows with same numbers in different suits.  \n- Pure Double Chow? Unlikely.  \n- Short Straight? Possibly.  \n- No Honors (1 point)  \n- Self Drawn (1 point)  \n- etc.  \n\nIt might be hard to reach 8 points. But maybe