es a lone tile.\n- Discard W4: But it's our only pair. Discarding a W4 would leave us with no pair, and we'd need to form a new pair from other tiles. Not good.\n- Discard T5: T5 can combine with T6 and T7 to form a chow if we break the T6 triplet? Actually we have T6 triplet, so we could use one T6 with T5 and T7 to form a chow, but then we'd have two T6 left which could be a pair. That would give us: chow(T5,T6,T7), pair(T6,T6), plus the other two chows, and we'd need one more meld from W2, W4, W4, B5. That seems messy. Alternatively, T5 could be part of a pung with two more T5 (3 left). So keeping T5 has potential.\n- Discard T6: We have three T6. Discarding one would leave two T6 as a pair, and we lose a pung. But we could then form a chow with T5,T6,T7 if we draw T7. But that would require drawing T7 and also having a pair elsewhere. Currently we have W4 pair, so discarding one T6 would give us: pair W4, pair T6, and singles W2, W4, T5, B5. That is two pairs, which is good for Seven Pairs possibility? But we have melds already exposed (two chows), so Seven Pairs is not possible because melds are exposed (Seven Pairs requires a concealed hand). So we cannot go for Seven Pairs. So discarding T6 breaks a ready meld (pung) and leaves us with less melds. Not advisable.\n- Discard B5: B5 is a single with potential for sequences (B3,B4,B5 or B4,B5,B6 or B5,B6,B7) or pung (4 B5 left). B5 is also a 5, which could be useful for \"All Fives\" if we have other 5s, but we have T5 and W5? We don't have W5. \"All Fives\" requires every meld and pair to contain a 5. That's unlikely. Discarding B5 removes a lone tile from a different suit, helping to reduce suits for \"One Voided Suit\" (having exactly two suits). Currently we have W, T, and B in hand. If we discard B5, we'd have only W and T (plus honors none), so that would satisfy \"One Voided Suit\" (2 points) if we win. That's a scoring pattern. Also, by discarding B5, we focus on W and T suits, which might be easier to form sequences since we have more tiles in those suits. \n\nConsidering scoring patterns: We have two chows already, both are simples (no terminals/honors). The T6 pung is also simple. The pair W4 is simple. So we have \"All Simples\" potential (2 points). Also, if we discard B5, we achieve \"One Voided Suit\" (2 points). That's 4 points already. We need at least 8 points to win. Additional patterns could include: \"All Chows\" (2 points) if our fourth meld is a chow (but we have a pung, so not all chows). \"Concealed Hand\" (2 points) if we win by self-draw? But we have exposed melds from Chi, so not concealed. \"Self Drawn\" (1 point). \"Pung of Terminals or Honors\" none. \"Short Straight\" maybe? We have two chows: T3,T4,T5 and W6,W7,W8. They are not in the same suit, so not Short Straight. \"Mixed Double Chow\"? Not applicable. \"Pure Double Chow\"? No. \"Tile Hog\"? We have T6 triplet, but not four identical. \"No Honors\" (1 point) likely. So current potential points: All Simples (2) + One Voided Suit if we discard B5 (2) + No Honors (1) = 5 points. Still need 3 more. Could get \"All Chows\" if we change T6 pung into a chow, but that's difficult. Alternatively, we could aim for \"Half Flush\" if we focus on one suit and honors, but we have two suits and no honors. Not likely.\n\nMaybe we should consider discarding W2 instead of B5? If we discard W2, we still have three suits (W, T, B), so no One Voided Suit. Discarding W2 leaves W4 pair, T5, T6 triplet, B5. Still three suits. Discarding T5 leaves W2, W4 pair, T6 triplet, B5. Three suits. Discarding T6 as said breaks a meld. So discarding B5 seems best to reduce to two suits and aim for One Voided Suit.\n\nAlso, look at safety: Discard B5. What tiles are dangerous? From history, opponents have discarded various tiles. B5 has not been discarded recently? Looking at history: Previous player played B4, B8, B7, B9. Next player played B7, B3. Across played B8, B2, B1. B5 has not been discarded. But B5 is a middle tile, often safe early but may be dangerous later. However, we are not in late game? The wall tiles are many (unshown tiles list shows many left). So safety is moderate.\n\nWe also need to consider tile efficiency: Which discard leaves the best waiting shape? After discarding B5, we have: W2, W4, W4, T5, T6, T6, T6. We need one more meld. We can try to form a chow with W2,W3,W4 if we draw W3 (3 left), but that would use one W4 from the pair, breaking the pair. Then we'd have: chow(W2,W3,W4), leaving W4 single, T5, T6 triplet. Then we need a pair and a meld. The pair could be W4 if we draw another W4, or T5 if we draw another T5, or use T6 pair from triplet? But triplet is a meld, so if we break it, we lose a meld. Alternatively, we could aim for a meld from T5 and T6: if we draw T7, we can form chow T5,T6,T7 using one T6 from the triplet, leaving two T6 as a pair. That would give us: existing two chows, plus chow(T5,T6,T7), plus pung(T6)?? Wait, we used one T6 for the chow, so the triplet becomes a pair of T6 and one extra T6? Actually we have three T6. If we use one T6 in a chow with T5 and T7, we have two T6 left, which can be a pair. And the chow T5,T6,T7 becomes a new meld. Then we have: melds: chow1, chow2, chow(T5,T6,T7), pung(T6)?? No, we used one T6 in chow, so the remaining two T6 are a pair, not a pung. So we would have three chows and one pair, and we need one more meld. That meld could come from W tiles: we have W2 and W4. If we draw W3, we can form chow W2,W3,W4, but that would use W4 from the pair? Actually we have W4 pair. If we use one W4 in chow, we break the pair. Then we have chow W2,W3,W4, leaving a single W4. Then we need a pair from somewhere else. We could pair T5 if we draw another T5, or pair W4 if we draw another W4. So it's complicated.\n\nMaybe we should consider discarding W2 instead? After discarding W2: hand is W4 pair, T5, T6 triplet, B5. We need a meld from T5 and B5? Not together. We could try to get a meld in B suit with B5 (draw B3,B4 or B4,B6 or B6,B7) or pung B5. Or meld in T with T5 (draw T3,T4 or T4,T6 or T6,T7) or pung T5. Or meld in W with W4 (pung W4). So we have more flexibility with two suits (W and T) and one B. But still three suits.\n\nGiven the scoring potential, discarding B5 to achieve One Voided Suit seems promising. Also, it simplifies the hand by removing the odd suit. We can then focus on forming sequences in W and T. The T6 triplet is a solid meld, so we only need one more meld and a pair (we already have W4 pair). So we need one more meld from W2, W4, T5. Possibilities: \n- Draw W3 to complete chow W2,W3,W4 (using one W4 from pair, then we need to find a new pair).\n- Draw W4 to complete pung W4 (then we have pair from somewhere else, maybe T5 or draw a pair).\n- Draw T4 to complete chow T4,T5,T6 (using one T6 from triplet? But that breaks the triplet, not good).\n- Draw T7 to complete chow T5,T6,T7 (using one T6 from triplet, again breaks triplet).\n- Draw T5 to complete pung T5 (then we have pair W4).\n- Draw B5? But we discarded B5, so not.\nSo the best draws are W3, W4, T5. All have several tiles left.\n\nThus, discarding B5 seems reasonable.\n\nReason: Discarding B5 reduces the hand to two suits (W and T), which qualifies for the \"One Voided Suit\" pattern (2 points) if we win, and it's a safe discard given it's a middle tile not heavily discarded. It keeps the T6 pung intact and maintains the W4 pair, leaving us one meld away from a winning hand with good tile efficiency (waiting on W3, W4, T5). Other discards either break a meld (T6) or keep three suits, reducing scoring potential.\n\nAnswer: Play B5"}
